---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> A2: Briefly describe what happens in a call to timer_sleep(),
>> including the effects of the timer interrupt handler.

timer_sleep() returns immediately if the duration is zero or negative.
Otherwise, it initializes a local semaphore to zero. With interrupts
disabled, it calculates the wakeup tick and inserts the request into
the sorted sleep list. It then restores interrupts and calls sema_down()
to block.

The timer interrupt checks the front of the list. It removes each
request whose wakeup tick has arrived and calls sema_up() on its
semaphore. It stops at the first request that is not due. This wakes
all threads due on the same tick.

>> A3: What steps are taken to minimize the amount of time spent in
>> the timer interrupt handler?

The list is sorted when a thread goes to sleep, so the interrupt
handler only visits requests that are due, plus the first one that
is not. It does not scan the rest of the list. Inserting a request
costs O(n); waking k requests costs O(k + 1).

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A4: How are race conditions avoided when multiple threads call
>> timer_sleep() simultaneously?

Each thread has its own request and semaphore. Interrupts are disabled
only while reading ticks and inserting into the shared list. On this
single-CPU system, another thread cannot run during those operations.
The caller restores interrupts before waiting on its semaphore.

>> A5: How are race conditions avoided when a timer interrupt occurs
>> during a call to timer_sleep()?

The request is initialized before it is added to the list. Interrupts
are disabled during insertion, so the handler cannot see an unfinished
request.

The timer may signal after interrupts are restored but before
sema_down(). In that case, sema_up() leaves the semaphore value at one,
and sema_down() consumes it without blocking. If sema_down() runs first,
the thread blocks until the timer signals it.

The handler removes the request before signaling. Its stack storage
stays valid while timer_sleep() is blocked, and it is no longer in the
list when the function returns.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A6: Why did you choose this design?  In what ways is it superior to
>> another design you considered?

A semaphore lets each thread block and handles a signal that arrives
before the thread starts waiting. Keeping the request on the stack
avoids allocating memory or adding fields to struct thread.

An unsorted list would make insertion faster, but the timer would have
to scan every request on every tick. Sorting at insertion keeps that
work out of the interrupt handler. The cost is O(n) insertion with
interrupts disabled, which gets more expensive as the list grows.

