>> B2: Explain the data structure used to track priority donation.
>> Use ASCII art to diagram a nested donation.  (Alternately, submit a
>> .png file.)

held_locks groups donations by the lock being requested. Each lock has
a contenders list, and each contender's waiting_lock points to that lock.
Together with lock.holder, these links describe the donation chain.

A contender stays on this list until it acquires the lock. This matters
because sema_up() can make it ready before it has actually taken the lock;
it still needs to donate during that interval.

For H, M, and L with base priorities 50, 30, and 10:

    H (base 50)           M (base 30)           L (base 10)
    waits for A -------> holds A
                         waits for B -------> holds B
    effective 50         effective 50         effective 50

A holder's effective priority is the maximum of its base priority and
the effective priorities of contenders for all its held locks. Donation
follows waiting_lock -> holder, stopping after eight holders, a null
holder, or a repeated thread.

---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> B3: How do you ensure that the highest priority thread waiting for
>> a lock, semaphore, or condition variable wakes up first?

Ready threads and semaphore waiters are appended at the tail. Selection
uses the first maximum of their current effective priorities. This keeps
equal-priority threads in FIFO order and handles a donation received
while a thread is already waiting.

sema_up() selects, removes, and unblocks the highest-priority waiter, then
increments the semaphore before allowing preemption. Locks use this same semaphore
rule. cond_signal() selects through the saved thread pointer. Looking
inside its private semaphore is unsafe because the signal can arrive
before sema_down(). An early signal stays in the semaphore; a blocked
waiter becomes ready. cond_wait() reacquires the monitor lock on return.

A higher-priority ready thread triggers a yield after the caller finishes
its state changes. thread_create() performs the same check for a new
thread. Idle gives way to any ready thread, including priority zero.
thread_unblock() keeps its atomic contract in thread context, so its
caller checks preemption after finishing. Interrupt handlers request
intr_yield_on_return() instead of switching inside the handler.

>> B4: Describe the sequence of events when a call to lock_acquire()
>> causes a priority donation.  How is nested donation handled?

If the lock is busy, lock_acquire() sets waiting_lock, registers the
caller in contenders, and recomputes the holder's effective priority.
If that holder is waiting for another lock, the update continues along
the holder chain, up to eight holders. sema_down() then blocks the caller
until it can take the semaphore.

After success, the caller removes its contender entry if it registered
one, clears waiting_lock, and becomes the holder. The lock is added to
held_locks, and the new holder's priority is recomputed from remaining
contenders. Ownership bookkeeping also applies to an uncontended acquire
and a successful lock_try_acquire(). A failed try registers no wait or
donation. The list and priority changes occur within the synchronization
primitives' short atomic sections.

>> B5: Describe the sequence of events when lock_release() is called
>> on a lock that a higher-priority thread is waiting for.

The releasing thread removes the lock from held_locks and clears its
holder. It recomputes its effective priority from base_priority and the
contenders for its remaining locks. Donations for the released lock are
removed; donations for other locks still count.

sema_up() then unblocks the highest-priority waiter and releases the
semaphore. The next holder recomputes its priority from the remaining
contenders when acquisition succeeds. Once release bookkeeping is
complete, the caller yields if a higher-priority thread is ready.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B6: Describe a potential race in thread_set_priority() and explain
>> how your implementation avoids it.  Can you use a lock to avoid
>> this race?

Suppose thread_set_priority() reads the donation list and is preempted
before writing its result. Another thread adds a higher donation. When
the setter resumes, its stale result could overwrite the new priority.

The base-priority change, donation scan, effective-priority write, and
ready-priority comparison belong in one short atomic update. An active
donation can keep the effective priority above the new base value. Any
yield follows the completed update; thread_get_priority() returns the
effective value.

A sleeping lock would create another scheduling and donation dependency
inside the mechanism being updated, and an interrupt handler cannot
acquire it. Short interrupt exclusion protects this internal state on
Pintos's single CPU. Actual waits still use semaphores and locks.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B7: Why did you choose this design?  In what ways is it superior to
>> another design you considered?

Tracking contenders by lock makes it easy to remove just the donations
associated with a released lock. Selecting by current priority also
avoids moving a thread whenever a donation changes its position in a
ready or waiting queue.

An ordered queue would make selection cheaper, but every priority change
would require fixing the thread's position, even when it is blocked on
another semaphore. Our list-based design has fewer update paths. Its
cost is linear selection, scans of held locks and contenders, and a few
extra list links per thread and lock.

