>> C3: Did any ambiguities in the scheduler specification make values
>> in the table uncertain?  If so, what rule did you use to resolve
>> them?  Does this match the behavior of your scheduler?

A, B, and C start ready in that order and remain runnable. Each row
shows values after the listed tick's accounting and priority update.
The final column gives the thread selected for the next four-tick slice.

At slice expiry, the running thread joins the ready-list tail. The first
maximum priority wins. Thus B precedes A at tick 8, and C precedes both
at tick 24. A is selected again at tick 4 because its priority is still
higher. These conventions are also the rules for the planned scheduler.
With TIMER_FREQ=100, no one-second update occurs in this table.

>> C4: How is the way you divided the cost of scheduling between code
>> inside and outside interrupt context likely to affect performance?

Every tick adds one recent_cpu unit to the running non-idle thread,
then wakes due sleepers. Every TIMER_FREQ ticks, load_avg is updated
before recent_cpu is recomputed for all non-idle threads, including
blocked threads. Every fourth global tick updates their priorities.
When both boundaries coincide, the priority formula uses the new values.

    priority = PRI_MAX - recent_cpu / 4 - 2 * nice
    load_avg = (59/60) * load_avg + (1/60) * ready_threads
    recent_cpu = (2*load_avg)/(2*load_avg+1) * recent_cpu + nice

ready_threads counts the ready list plus the running non-idle thread,
after alarm wakeups. The updates finish before interrupt return. This
requires splitting thread_tick() into accounting and periodic updates,
with the latter called after the alarm wake loop. After the updates, the
handler compares the current and ready priorities and requests
intr_yield_on_return() when needed.

Periodic updates cost O(n) for n threads. Dispatch costs O(r) for r ready
threads, and waking k sleepers adds O(k+1). All-thread scans occur only
at the specified boundaries. Thread creation and thread_set_nice()
calculate one thread's priority outside interrupt context, with short
atomic sections for shared state.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> C5: Briefly critique your design, pointing out advantages and
>> disadvantages in your design choices.  If you were to have extra
>> time to work on this part of the project, how might you choose to
>> refine or improve your design?

One ready list keeps selection simple and works with both schedulers.
Reading priorities at dispatch avoids an ordering that becomes stale
after a periodic update. Blocked threads receive updated values without
being moved between queues.

The main costs are O(r) dispatch and O(n) periodic scans. With more time,
we would consider 64 FIFO ready queues to reduce dispatch cost. That
would require moving ready threads after priority changes and preserving
the same tie order. For this project, the single list is the simpler
starting point.

>> C6: The assignment explains arithmetic for fixed-point math in
>> detail, but it leaves it open to you to implement it.  Why did you
>> decide to implement it the way you did?  If you created an
>> abstraction layer for fixed-point math, that is, an abstract data
>> type and/or a set of functions or macros to manipulate fixed-point
>> numbers, why did you do so?  If not, why not?

We plan to put small inline helpers in threads/fixed-point.h using
signed 17.14 values and F = 1 << 14. Naming the operations keeps the
scale conversions in one place and makes the scheduler formulas easier
to check.

An integer n becomes n * F, and adding it to x uses x + n * F. Two
fixed-point values multiply as ((int64_t) x * y) / F and divide as
((int64_t) x * F) / y. The wide intermediate prevents scaling overflow.
Multiplication and division by F also handle negative operands without
relying on signed shifts.

Stored values keep their fractions, including negative recent_cpu.
Priority is calculated in fixed point, then converted toward zero and
clamped to 0..63. For a valid positive priority, this rounds down. The
recent_cpu/4 term is not truncated early. In the one-second update, the
coefficient is calculated before multiplying it by recent_cpu.

The load and recent CPU getters multiply by 100 in a wide intermediate
and round to nearest. For scaled x, use (x + F/2)/F when x is nonnegative
and (x - F/2)/F when negative, with division toward zero. Separate helpers
for truncation and rounding make that distinction explicit.

